Name the upper objects $A,B,C$ and the lower objects $D,E,F$, so the <morphisms> are $b:A\to B$, $c:B\to C$, $a:A\to D$, $g:B\to E$, $d:C\to F$, $f:D\to E$, and $e:E\to F$. The <commutative diagram> gives $gb=fa$ and $dc=eg$.

Take $u:T\to D$ and $v:T\to C$ with $efu=dv$. The right <pullback in a category> gives a unique $w:T\to B$ with $gw=fu$ and $cw=v$. The left <pullback in a category> then gives a unique $z:T\to A$ with $az=u$ and $bz=w$. It follows that $cbz=v$, giving the required factorization through the outside rectangle. Any other factorization has the same middle <morphism> by uniqueness in the right <pullback in a category>, and therefore is the same $z$ by uniqueness in the left <pullback in a category>. **The outside rectangle is a pullback**.

= ii
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Keep the notation of the preceding part. For $u:T\to D$ and $w:T\to B$ satisfying $fu=gw$, we have $efu=egw=dcw$. The outside <pullback in a category> gives a unique $z:T\to A$ with $az=u$ and $cbz=cw$. Moreover $gbz=faz=fu=gw$. The <morphisms> $bz,w:T\to B$ thus have the same composites with $g$ and $c$. Uniqueness in the right <pullback in a category> gives $bz=w$. Any factorization through the left square also factors through the outside rectangle, so it is unique there. **The left square is a pullback**. Together these two arguments prove the <pullback pasting lemma>.

= iii
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Here is a precise <base change of a pullback square> statement. Suppose $A=B\times_D C$ is a <pullback in a category> and $t:D'\to D$ is a <morphism>. Assume the <pullbacks in a category>
$$
A'=A\times_D D',\quad B'=B\times_D D',\quad C'=C\times_D D'
$$
exist, where $A\to D$ is the common composite. The induced square with vertices $A',B',C',D'$ is a <pullback in a category>; equivalently,
$$
\boxed{(B\times_D C)\times_D D'\cong(B\times_D D')\times_{D'}(C\times_D D').}
$$
Indeed, compatible <morphisms> $T\to B'$ and $T\to C'$ give <morphisms> $b:T\to B$, $c:T\to C$, and a common $v:T\to D'$, with the composites of $b,c$ to $D$ both equal to $tv$. The original <pullback in a category> gives a unique <morphism> $h:T\to A$ with projections $b,c$. Its composite to $D$ is $tv$, so the defining <pullback in a category> for $A'$ gives a unique <morphism> $T\to A'$ above $h,v$. Its maps to $B'$ and $C'$ are the prescribed ones by uniqueness in those <pullbacks in a category>. Conversely, these projections recover $b,c,v$, so every possible factorization is the same. This proves the <universal property> without requiring arbitrary <categorical limits> in the ambient <category>.

