= Solution
{parent=i}

For $D:\mathcal I\to\mathcal C$, define the <functor> $\operatorname{Cone}(-,D):\mathcal C^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathbf{Set}$ by
$$
\operatorname{Cone}(X,D)=\operatorname{Nat}(\Delta X,D),
$$
where $\Delta X$ is the <constant diagram functor>; a <morphism> into $X$ acts by precomposition on every <categorical cone> leg. A <categorical limit> is a <representation of a functor> of the form
$$
\boxed{\mathcal C(-,L)\cong\operatorname{Cone}(-,D).}
$$
The image of $1_L$ is the universal <categorical cone> $\lambda:\Delta L\to D$. By <naturality> of the representing <isomorphism>, the image of $h:X\to L$ is $\lambda\circ\Delta h$. Thus this definition is exactly existence and uniqueness of factorization of every <categorical cone> through $\lambda$. Conversely that <universal property> gives the representing <natural bijection>.

= ii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Choose for each $j\in\mathcal J$ a <categorical limit> $L_j$ of $F(-,j)$, with projections $\lambda_{i,j}:L_j\to F(i,j)$. For $v:j\to k$, the <morphisms> $F(1_i,v)\lambda_{i,j}$ form a <categorical cone> over $F(-,k)$, since the two actions of the product <functor> $F$ commute. Define $L(v):L_j\to L_k$ by the equations
$$
\lambda_{i,k}L(v)=F(1_i,v)\lambda_{i,j}\qquad(i\in\mathcal I).
$$
Existence and uniqueness follow from the <universal property>. The <identity morphism> of $L_j$ satisfies the equations for $v=1_j$, and $L(w)L(v)$ satisfies those for $wv$, so uniqueness gives $L(1_j)=1_{L_j}$ and $L(wv)=L(w)L(v)$. Hence $L:\mathcal J\to\mathcal D$ is a <functor>.

If $L'_j$ with projections $\lambda'_{i,j}$ are other choices, there is a unique <isomorphism> $u_j:L_j\to L'_j$ carrying every projection to the corresponding projection. The same equations show $u_kL(v)=L'(v)u_j$, because their composites with all $\lambda'_{i,k}$ agree. Thus the choices give **a unique <natural isomorphism> compatible with the chosen <categorical limit> <categorical cones>**. Without the compatibility condition, uniqueness of an arbitrary <natural isomorphism> is not asserted.

= iii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Let $M$ be a <categorical limit> of $L:\mathcal J\to\mathcal D$, with projections $p_j:M\to L_j$. <Set> $\gamma_{i,j}=\lambda_{i,j}p_j$. The equations for the <categorical cones> $\lambda$ and $p$, and the defining equations for $L(v)$, show that this family is a <categorical cone> over $F:\mathcal I\times\mathcal J\to\mathcal D$.

For any <categorical cone> $x_{i,j}:X\to F(i,j)$, fix $j$. Its $\mathcal I$-legs give a unique $h_j:X\to L_j$. For $v:j\to k$, both $L(v)h_j$ and $h_k$ have composite $x_{i,k}$ with every $\lambda_{i,k}$, so they are equal. Thus the $h_j$ form a <categorical cone> over $L$, which factors uniquely through $h:X\to M$. The equalities $\gamma_{i,j}h=x_{i,j}$ follow. Any other such $h$ has the same composites with every $p_j$, because the projections $\lambda_{i,j}$ detect equality; uniqueness for $M$ then makes it equal to $h$. Therefore **$M$ is a <categorical limit> of the whole product <diagram in a category>**.

= iv
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

A precise <commutation of iterated categorical limits> statement is the following. For small <categories> $\mathcal I,\mathcal J$ and $F:\mathcal I\times\mathcal J\to\mathcal D$, if every column <categorical limit> and the resulting $\mathcal J$-limit exist, then
$$
\lim_j\lim_iF(i,j)\cong\lim_{(i,j)}F(i,j).
$$
If every row <categorical limit> and the resulting $\mathcal I$-limit also exist, then
$$
\boxed{\lim_j\lim_iF(i,j)\cong\lim_{(i,j)}F(i,j)\cong\lim_i\lim_jF(i,j).}
$$
Each <isomorphism> is the unique one identifying all projections to $F(i,j)$. Indeed, the preceding part proves that each iterated <categorical limit> has the <universal property> of the whole product <diagram in a category>. Its uniqueness gives the canonical <isomorphism>. Equivalently, <categorical cones> with vertex $X$ over the product <diagram in a category> are exactly compatible families, indexed by $j$, of <categorical cones> over $F(-,j)$; regrouping the same compatibility equations cannot change their representing object. In a <complete category> all the stated existence hypotheses hold for small $\mathcal I,\mathcal J$. The theorem is about <categorical limits> with <categorical limits>, and makes no unconditional claim about commuting a <categorical limit> with a <colimit>.

